# Git: use the current branch by default

When the user says **commit**, **push**, **merge**, **clean**, or similar without naming a branch:

- Do it on the **branch they are currently on**
- Do **not** create a new feature branch
- Do **not** open a PR to main unless they ask
- If they are on `main`, commit and push to `main`

When they name a target (**main**, another branch, or “merge into X”):

- Use that named branch / destination instead

## Examples

- On `main`, “commit and push” → commit on `main`, push `origin main`
- On `fix/foo`, “commit, push, merge” → commit/push `fix/foo`; merge only if they said merge (into the branch they named, or as they specified)
- “merge to main” / “push to main” → target `main` even if currently on another branch

## Do not

- Spin up a side branch just because they asked to commit or push
- Assume every push needs a PR into `main`
